from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "4. Tongues in Acts: Three Instances"),
("p", "Claim: the book of Acts reports tongues three times with the verb, and the three instances are given to Israel, to the Gentiles of Cornelius’s house in the presence of the circumcision, and to twelve disciples of John at Ephesus. The volume records them as the text records them and notes what each passage says about the hearers. It does not re-argue the Spirit’s coming, which belongs to Acts Commentary Volume 2."),
("sub", "4.1 Pentecost, Acts 2"),
("p", "The Spirit comes in Jerusalem. “And they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and began to speak with other tongues, as the Spirit gave them utterance” (Acts 2:4). Luke names the hearers: “there were dwelling at Jerusalem Jews, devout men, out of every nation under heaven” (Acts 2:5). They are confounded, “because that every man heard them speak in his own language” (Acts 2:6). The question they ask is, “how hear we every man in our own tongue, wherein we were born?” (Acts 2:8). The tongues are languages, and the hearers are Jews of the dispersion."),
("p", "Peter answers the multitude and addresses them: “Ye men of Judaea, and all ye that dwell at Jerusalem” (Acts 2:14). The scene is set in Jerusalem, at the feast, for Israel."),
("sub", "4.2 The house of Cornelius, Acts 10"),
("p", "Peter is preaching when “the Holy Ghost fell on all them which heard the word” (Acts 10:44). The witnesses are named: “they of the circumcision which believed were astonished, as many as came with Peter, because that on the Gentiles also was poured out the gift of the Holy Ghost” (Acts 10:45). The reason they know it is given: “For they heard them speak with tongues, and magnify God” (Acts 10:46). Peter’s reply is to ask, “Can any man forbid water, that these should not be baptized, which have received the Holy Ghost as well as we?” (Acts 10:47)."),
("p", "The passage says the circumcision who came with Peter were astonished at the gift to the Gentiles, and that the tongues were the evidence they saw. The volume reads the scene as an event of Israel’s Prophecy Program within the overlap, in which Gentiles hear through Peter. It notes that the tongues are heard by Jews as the sign that the Spirit has been given, and that Peter later relates the matter to the church at Jerusalem."),
("sub", "4.3 Ephesus, Acts 19"),
("p", "Paul comes to Ephesus and finds “certain disciples” (Acts 19:1). He asks them, “Have ye received the Holy Ghost since ye believed?” (Acts 19:2). They answer that they have not heard whether there is any Holy Ghost, and that they were baptized “Unto John’s baptism” (Acts 19:3). Paul tells them that John baptized “with the baptism of repentance, saying unto the people, that they should believe on him which should come after him, that is, on Christ Jesus” (Acts 19:4). They were baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus. Then: “And when Paul had laid his hands upon them, the Holy Ghost came on them; and they spake with tongues, and prophesied” (Acts 19:6). “And all the men were about twelve” (Acts 19:7)."),
("p", "The event stands in Acts 9 to 28 and is read as the volume reads every event of that period: it is recorded in a setting shaped by Israel’s Prophecy Program, which remained operative throughout Acts 9 to 28 even as the Mystery Program began. Where Paul or his companions are the ones working or ministering, the volume reports the text and does not assign the event to a single program. The disciples at Ephesus are disciples of John’s baptism, and tongues and prophecy are named together."),
("tbl", "The three instances of tongues in Acts.", [
    ("Acts 2:4, 6", T("Acts 2:4, 6"), "Jerusalem; devout Jews of every nation; the hearers understood in their own languages."),
    ("Acts 10:44–46", T("Acts 10:44–46"), "The house of Cornelius; the circumcision who came with Peter were astonished; the tongues were the evidence."),
    ("Acts 19:6, 7", T("Acts 19:6, 7"), "Ephesus; about twelve disciples of John’s baptism; tongues and prophecy named together."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "4.4 Peter reports Cornelius, Acts 11 and 15"),
("p", "Peter gives an account of the house of Cornelius twice. To the church at Jerusalem he says, “And as I began to speak, the Holy Ghost fell on them, as on us at the beginning” (Acts 11:15). He recalls the word of the Lord: “John indeed baptized with water; but ye shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost” (Acts 11:16). And he concludes, “Forasmuch then as God gave them the like gift as he did unto us, who believed on the Lord Jesus Christ; what was I, that I could withstand God?” (Acts 11:17). At the council he says that “God, which knoweth the hearts, bare them witness, giving them the Holy Ghost, even as he did unto us” (Acts 15:8)."),
("p", "Peter’s two accounts name the gift as the Holy Ghost given “as on us at the beginning,” and the word “witness” in 15:8 says that the giving was a testimony. The accounts speak of the gift and do not repeat the word tongues. The volume reports this and draws no conclusion from the omission. It notes that Peter gives the event as a witness before the circumcision, in the setting of the Jerusalem church, where the question was whether Gentiles could be received."),
("sub", "4.5 What the three instances share"),
("p", "In each of the three passages the speaking in tongues follows the coming of the Holy Ghost, in each it is reported with the same verb phrase, and in none of them does the narrator state who the languages were spoken to or for what purpose beyond what the context says. At Pentecost the hearers understand. At Cornelius’s house the witnesses are Jewish believers who were astonished. At Ephesus the witnesses are the twelve men themselves and Paul. The volume does not add a purpose that the narrator does not state. It notes only that Acts places these instances in a setting of Israel’s ministry, or of Paul’s ministry in an overlap shaped by Israel’s program, and that Paul’s own statement of the purpose, in 1 Corinthians 14:22, is in the letter and not in Acts."),
("p", "The book of Acts does not report tongues at the conversion of the Samaritans in chapter 8, and it does not say that they spoke with tongues. The volume does not draw a conclusion from silence. It notes that Acts 8:17 reports the Spirit given, that Acts 9:17 reports Paul filled, and that neither verse mentions tongues."),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Acts reports tongues at Jerusalem, at Cornelius’s house and at Ephesus, and the hearers at Pentecost understand the speech as their own languages.",
    "At Cornelius’s house the circumcision who came with Peter are the witnesses, and they were astonished.",
    "At Ephesus the speakers are disciples of John’s baptism, tongues and prophecy are named together, and the event is reported without assignment to a single program.",
    "None of the three passages states a purpose beyond its context. Paul states the purpose in 1 Corinthians 14:22.",
  ]),
]
